Let pace ranks: 0=fastest, 1=mid, 2=slowest. A "normal" grid (fastest at front) is [0,1,2]; a static finish likely follows [0,1,2]. A reverse grid is [2,1,0]. We want to avoid [0,1,2] (fastest at the front with no one to pass) but allow other permutations. For n=3, there are 6 permutations; reject those where rank 0 is at grid 0? That leaves 4 permutations. E.g., [1,2,0], [2,1,0], [1,0,2], [2,0,1]. In [1,0,2], the fastest starts P2 and only has the mid ahead; the slowest is P3 and won't be passed by the fastest (already behind them). The finish would be [0,2,1]? Actually the fastest passes the mid, finishing [0,1,2]... no, the slowest P3 stays P3, mid P1→P2, fastest P2→P1. Final [fast, mid, slow] which differs from grid [mid, fast, slow] ✓. So rejecting the "fastest on pole" permutation guarantees at least one position change (fastest must pass someone).

