Confirmed: with a single AI, `slotDist: 0` and it starts at the start line instead of behind it, and lateral 0.96 not ±2.75. The grid hole bug. Also, it ran 9.15 laps because state stayed 'racing' (player parked, never finishes) — my loop ended at 20000 ticks (666s). Fine.

Fix the grid allocation. Let me rewrite that block with a clean, hole-free assignment:

```js
const byPace = [...r.ai].sort((a, b) => b.pace - a.pace);
const slots = shuffleInPlace(Array.from({ length: r.ai.length }, (_, i) => i));
const heldBack = r.ai.length >= 3 ? Math.floor(r.ai.length / 3) : 0;
const gridOrder = new Array(r.ai.length);
// quickest cars go to the back half in random order
byPace.slice(0, heldBack).forEach((a, i) => { gridOrder[slots[heldBack + i]] = a; });
// everyone else fills the rest
byPace.slice(heldBack).forEach((a, i) => { gridOrder[slots[i]] = a; });
```

Wait: slots is a shuffled array of all slot indices. Assign heldBack quickest to slots[heldBack..] and the rest to slots[0..heldBack-1]. That gives a random permutation where quickest third land in back slots and others in front slots. No holes because all slots indices used exactly once. Yes.

For length 2: heldBack=0; byPace all to slots[0..1] shuffled. Random. Good. For length 1: heldBack 0 → slots[0]. Good.

Then also, the player grid slot: r.ai.length. Fine.

Now about pace distribution: pace ladder random assignment independent of grid. Good.

Next issue: AI cars finishing. The player's pace (57 m/s avg vs AI 49) — hmm, wait AI dist at 100s = 4902 → average 49 m/s but that includes 0.15-0.75s start delay and launch ramp. Still base speed profile max 84 in straights, but corner speeds 19-40. Player auto-drive max 84 as well but lateral 31 vs AI cornering 24. On a track with many corners, that's a 14% corner-speed difference.

Should I retune AI to be closer to the player's autodrive? For a "pro" difficulty, AI should be competitive with a player using auto-drive (the game auto-drives throttle; only steering is the player's job). The player's theoretical pace is based on lateral 31. If AI corner at lateral 24 * diff.factor 0.915 * pace 0.945-1.048, effective lateral = 24*0.837–0.958 = ~20-23 vs player 31. So AI is much slower. On screenshots earlier perhaps this was accepted, but requirement is "opponents race for position, overtake, don't just finish where they started" - the race can still be fine if AI are slower, but it's better if they're competitive.

Let me measure AI race pace vs player's actual race pace on the same track from the full race: player finished 5700m in 99.75s → 57.1 m/s. AI leader (RUSH) at 100.2s had 4902 → 48.9 m/s. That's 14% slower. So player wins by ~16s over 3 laps. Reasonable margin maybe too easy, but "pro" difficulty. The old code had factor 0.915 and pace 0.968-1.032 → similar or slower. Not my new regression.

Focus: overtaking among AI. Need a test with 6 AI. Let's run ai-sim 6 3.

